// SR-PERM-06, SR-PERM-07, SR-PERM-08: the `from=` restriction -- the only place where an identity
// decides *where* a peer may come from. Models identity_options.go: matchesSourcePattern (an
// address, a CIDR block, or a glob over the textual address) and CheckSource (the list of
// possibly-negated patterns).
//
// Tracked files: identity_options.go (CheckSource, sourceMatches, matchesSourcePattern, parsePeerIP),
// source_check.go (sourceVerdictFor, negatedMatch, inclusionMatch, isNegated), glob.go (matchGlob),
// identity_options_source_conformance_test.go (the correspondence). The list decision proved below is
// now also proved on shipped code in source_check.go, so this unit is a second opinion on the same
// rule rather than the only argument for it; the address layer underneath it stays modelled here, as
// it can only be.
//
// The layers that consult `net` are modelled by the facts they return, because Gobra cannot ingest
// them: isCidr says "net.ParseCIDR accepted the pattern", contains says "the network holds the
// address", parsesAsIp says "net.ParseIP accepted it", ipEquals is net.IP.Equal, wildcard is
// strings.ContainsAny(pattern, "*?["), and globMatches is matchGlob. Each is a premise, so every
// lemma here holds whatever those layers decide -- and identity_options_source_conformance_test.go
// and identity_options_test.go say what they decide, over real CIDRs, the IPv4-in-IPv6 forms, real
// globs and host names.
//
// What is proved:
//   * fail-closed where it matters: an empty pattern matches nothing and an undeterminable address
//     matches nothing, before any of the three strategies is consulted;
//   * a CIDR pattern's verdict is exactly containment -- when ParseCIDR accepts a pattern nothing
//     else runs for it, so a valid network which does not hold the peer refuses instead of falling
//     through to the glob or to the literal comparison, which might have matched the text;
//   * a pattern naming a host (neither network, nor address, nor glob) matches nothing: no DNS
//     lookup is performed, and the absence of a lookup is a refusal rather than an open question;
//   * a literal address pattern is exactly net.IP.Equal, and the recursive glob is consulted only
//     for a pattern that really contains a wildcard -- so it never sees an address or a network,
//     where "*" and "?" would have no meaning;
//   * the `from=` list: no `from=` restricts nothing; under a restriction an undeterminable peer
//     address refuses; an exclusion refuses whatever else the list says, including a positive match
//     for the same address; and a peer accepted under a restriction is one some pattern described
//     and no negation excluded. Since the decision reads only the two disjunctions the loop
//     computes, the order of the list cannot change the answer -- which is what lets the loop walk
//     the whole list, and what makes from="a,!b" and from="!b,a" one restriction.
package sourcecheck

// matchGlob itself -- the recursive glob -- is deliberately NOT a Gobra function, and the reason
// is worth recording because it is a property of the prover, not of the code: in Gobra v26.09 a
// *pure* function defined by recursion is opaque. Its `decreases` measure is checked, but a call
// like gm("a", "a", 0, 0) cannot then be proved of it (nor gm("*", t) by induction), and indexing
// a slice inside a pure function additionally needs permissions for the elements. Recursion over
// plain integers does unfold -- `f(3)` and `h(2,2)` verify -- so arithmetic models like the
// throttle's belong here, and string-shaped ones do not. See proof/README.md, "Gotchas".
//
// What the glob means, and why it terminates, is therefore pinned where it can be executed:
// identity_options_source_conformance_test.go runs the real matchGlob against an independent
// linear-time matcher over every pattern and text of a corpus, and proves there that a
// wildcard-free pattern matches exactly the text equal to it (SR-PERM-07) and that the recursion
// cannot be made to run away by a pattern of alternating stars. What stays in this unit is
// everything the glob's result is *combined* with -- the three-strategy choice, the two guards,
// and the negated list -- which is where fail-closed lives.
//
// patternMatches is matchesSourcePattern, in the order the real function tries its three
// strategies (network, literal address, glob) and after its two guards.
decreases 0
pure func patternMatches(patternEmpty bool, addressKnown bool, isCidr bool, contains bool,
	wildcard bool, parsesAsIp bool, ipEquals bool, globMatches bool) bool {
	return !patternEmpty && addressKnown && ((isCidr && contains) ||
		(!isCidr && !wildcard && parsesAsIp && ipEquals) ||
		(!isCidr && wildcard && globMatches))
}

// guardsRefuseFirst: an empty pattern and an undeterminable address are refused before any of the
// three strategies is consulted. Both are fail-closed: nothing in the pattern can be worth a
// match if there is no pattern, and no pattern can be trusted against an address the server
// cannot name.
ensures !patternMatches(true, addressKnown, isCidr, contains, wildcard, parsesAsIp, ipEquals, globMatches)
ensures !patternMatches(patternEmpty, false, isCidr, contains, wildcard, parsesAsIp, ipEquals, globMatches)
func guardsRefuseFirst(patternEmpty bool, addressKnown bool, isCidr bool, contains bool,
	wildcard bool, parsesAsIp bool, ipEquals bool, globMatches bool) {
	assert !patternMatches(true, addressKnown, isCidr, contains, wildcard, parsesAsIp, ipEquals, globMatches)
	assert !patternMatches(patternEmpty, false, isCidr, contains, wildcard, parsesAsIp, ipEquals, globMatches)
}

// aCidrPatternIsDecidedByContainmentAlone: when net.ParseCIDR accepts the pattern, the real code
// returns network.Contains(ip) and nothing else runs. A valid network which does not hold the
// peer therefore refuses, instead of falling through to a glob or a literal comparison that might
// have matched the text by accident.
ensures !patternEmpty && addressKnown && isCidr ==> res == contains
func cidrIsDecidedByContainment(patternEmpty bool, addressKnown bool, isCidr bool, contains bool,
	wildcard bool, parsesAsIp bool, ipEquals bool, globMatches bool) (res bool) {
	res = patternMatches(patternEmpty, addressKnown, isCidr, contains, wildcard, parsesAsIp, ipEquals, globMatches)
}

// aHostnameMatchesNothing: a pattern that is not a network, holds no wildcard and does not parse
// as an address names a host, and no DNS lookup is performed. Not "matches whatever a resolver
// happens to answer" -- the absence of a lookup is a refusal, which is also why the code does not
// resolve and cannot be made to by a pattern.
ensures !isCidr && !wildcard && !parsesAsIp ==> !res
func hostnameMatchesNothing(patternEmpty bool, addressKnown bool, isCidr bool, contains bool,
	wildcard bool, parsesAsIp bool, ipEquals bool, globMatches bool) (res bool) {
	res = patternMatches(patternEmpty, addressKnown, isCidr, contains, wildcard, parsesAsIp, ipEquals, globMatches)
}

// aLiteralAddressIsEquality: with no wildcard and a pattern that parses as an address, the verdict
// is net.IP.Equal and nothing else. The one form of `from=` whose meaning is "this address", and
// it cannot be widened by anything the glob would have accepted -- which matters because the glob
// works on the *text*, and "[::1]" and "0:0:0:0:0:0:0:1" are the same address in two texts.
ensures !patternEmpty && addressKnown && !isCidr && !wildcard && parsesAsIp ==> res == ipEquals
func literalAddressIsEquality(patternEmpty bool, addressKnown bool, isCidr bool, contains bool,
	wildcard bool, parsesAsIp bool, ipEquals bool, globMatches bool) (res bool) {
	res = patternMatches(patternEmpty, addressKnown, isCidr, contains, wildcard, parsesAsIp, ipEquals, globMatches)
}

// theGlobRunsOnlyOnAGlob: matchGlob is consulted only for a pattern that is not a network and
// really contains one of "*?[" -- so the recursive matcher never sees an address or a CIDR, where
// those characters would have no meaning, and the verdict for a wildcard-free pattern is decided
// without it.
ensures addressKnown ==> res == (!patternEmpty && (!isCidr && !wildcard ==> parsesAsIp && ipEquals) && (isCidr ==> contains) && (!isCidr && wildcard ==> globMatches))
func globRunsOnlyOnAGlob(patternEmpty bool, addressKnown bool, isCidr bool, contains bool,
	wildcard bool, parsesAsIp bool, ipEquals bool, globMatches bool) (res bool) {
	res = patternMatches(patternEmpty, addressKnown, isCidr, contains, wildcard, parsesAsIp, ipEquals, globMatches)
}

// fromListDecides is CheckSource: no `from=` restricts nothing; otherwise the peer address must be
// determinable, some pattern must have described it, and no negated pattern may have matched. The
// real loop keeps walking after a positive match, so an exclusion anywhere in the list -- before
// or after -- is what decides.
decreases 0
pure func fromListDecides(restricted bool, addressKnown bool, positive bool, excluded bool) bool {
	return !restricted || addressKnown && !excluded && positive
}

ensures !restricted ==> fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, positive, excluded)
ensures restricted && !addressKnown ==> !fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, positive, excluded)
func failClosedOnUnknownSource(restricted bool, addressKnown bool, positive bool, excluded bool) {
	assert !restricted ==> fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, positive, excluded)
	assert restricted && !addressKnown ==> !fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, positive, excluded)
}

// anExclusionCannotBeOutvoted: from="10.0.0.0/8,!10.0.0.5" means what it looks like it means --
// the negation refuses the peer whatever the rest of the list says, including a positive match for
// the same address.
ensures restricted && excluded ==> !fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, true, excluded)
ensures restricted && excluded ==> !fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, false, excluded)
func exclusionCannotBeOutvoted(restricted bool, addressKnown bool, positive bool, excluded bool) {
	assert restricted && excluded ==> !fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, true, excluded)
}

// acceptedMeansDescribed: a peer accepted under a `from=` restriction is one some pattern of the
// list described and none of its negations excluded. There is no way in through an address that
// matches nothing, an excluded address, or an address the server could not determine.
ensures restricted && res ==> addressKnown && positive && !excluded
func acceptedMeansDescribed(restricted bool, addressKnown bool, positive bool, excluded bool) (res bool) {
	res = fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, positive, excluded)
}

// theVerdictDoesNotDependOnOrder: the decision reads the two disjunctions the loop computes over
// the list, nothing else, so swapping them -- which is what any permutation of the list amounts to
// -- cannot change the answer. This is why the loop can walk the whole list rather than stopping
// at the first match, and why from="a,!b" and from="!b,a" are the same restriction.
// anExclusionEndsTheWalkButChangesNothingElse: the real loop returns as soon as a negated pattern
// matches, so a positive pattern written after it is never examined. That is safe only because the
// verdict is refusal either way -- which is the same fact as exclusionCannotBeOutvoted, stated for
// the half where the order of the list is what one would worry about. It is why from="!b,a" and
// from="a,!b" are the same restriction, and the conformance test checks exactly that over real
// pattern lists in both orders.
ensures restricted && excluded ==> !fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, true, excluded)
ensures restricted && !excluded && positive && addressKnown ==> fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, positive, excluded)
func walkingStopsButTheVerdictStands(restricted bool, addressKnown bool, positive bool, excluded bool) {
	assert restricted && excluded ==> !fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, true, excluded)
	assert restricted && !excluded && positive && addressKnown ==>
		fromListDecides(restricted, addressKnown, positive, excluded)
}
